IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

PRINGSEWU REGENCY

LAMPUNG PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF PRINGSEWU REGENCY

NUMBER O7 OF 2015

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF PRINGSEWU,

Considering:

a. that every citizen has human rights that must be guaranteed by the state to obtain a sense of security, be protected and be free from all forms of violence.
b. that women and children are vulnerable groups, therefore they need to receive protection and services from the state that can provide prevention, protection and services to victims of violence;
c. that the regional government together with the community must carry out prevention, protection and recovery efforts for children and women victims of violence for the sake of honor and protection of human dignity;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number i 7 of 1984 concerning the ratification of the Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 5 of 1998 concerning the Ratification of the Convention Against Torture an~ Other Cruel, Inhuman or Degrading Treatment or Punishment (Convention Against Torture and Other Cruel, Inhuman or Degrading Treatment or Punishment) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 164, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3783);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 1651 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning the Ratification of ILO Convention No. 182 Concerning The Prohibition and Immediate of The Worst Forms of Child Labour (ILO Convention No. 182 concerning the Prohibition and Immediate Action for the Elimination of the Worst Forms of Child Labor) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3941);
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419); I
7. Law Number . 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
8. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 48 of 2008 concerning the Establishment of Pringsewu Regency (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 185, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4932);
10. Law Number 36 of 2009 Concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 44, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
11. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
12. Law Number 10 of 2012 concerning the Ratification of the Optional Protocol to The Convention on The Rights of The Child on The Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Potnography (Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child on the Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Pornography (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 149, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5330);
13. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
14. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 20141 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 246, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5589);
15. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation of Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence ~State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 8 of 2012 concerning Guidelines for Strengthening Dasawisma Groups for Prevention and Early Handling of Acts of Violence Against Children (State News Number 1088 of 2012);
17. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 1 of 2014 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products;
18. Regional Regulation of Pringsewu Regency Number O1 of 2010 concerning Government Affairs of Pringsewu Regency [Regional Gazette of Pringsewu Regency of 2010 Number 01);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PRINGSEWU REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF PRINGSEWU

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 4

The implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence aims to:
a. prevent all forms of acts of violence including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for victims of acts of violence;
d. provide services to victims of violence; and
e. carry out comprehensive recovery for victims.

CHAPTER IV

FORMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 6

Forms of violence include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/or
f. other violence.

Article 9

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 6 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or;
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and or certain purposes.

Article 10

Neglect as referred to in Article 6 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the unfulfillment of a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or manage as appropriate committed by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a person within the household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or;
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 13

Every victim has the right to obtain:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of rights related to the victim's status as a family member and community member.

Article 14

In addition to the rights as referred to in Article 13, child victims of violence also receive special rights including:
a. the right to respect for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services; 1c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and/or
e. the right to obtain freedom;
f. the right of victims and families to obtain facilities in the judicial process.

CHAPTER VII

INSTITUTIONS PROVIDING PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS

Article 16

1. The Regional Government establishes an Integrated Service Center (PPT) as the Implementing, Prevention and Protection Institution for Women and Children Victims of Violence in the region.
2. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of elements:
a. health;
b. psychologist;
c. law enforcement;
d. social and empowerment;
e. education and religion;
f. non-governmental organizations; and
g. private sector.
3. Further provisions regarding PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER VIII

PREVENTION OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 17

1. The Government in an effort to prevent acts of violence against women and children carries out empowerment of families, parents, communities by providing information, guidance and or counseling.
2. In addition to empowerment and support as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government carries out efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. striving to create employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern for prevention and from acts of violence against women and children;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and or care about women and children victims of violence.

Article 18

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in article 16, is carried out by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. education;
d. employment;
e. women's empowerment and child protection;
f. mental;
g. security and order;
2. Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on existing regulations.

CHAPTER XII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 23

1. The community participates in the implementation of protection for victims.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is in the form of:
a. forming family partners at the village/sub-district level by the community;
b. forming women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting socialization of women's and children's rights independently;
d. providing first aid to victims; ande. reporting to the relevant agency if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
